
====================================================================================================
S1. PRIOR ART, part 1 -- 'modified gravity fakes effective phantom' is long established
====================================================================================================
   * arXiv:0909.0351                    effective phantom behaviour in f(R) gravity, no fundamental phantom field
   * arXiv:1204.0369                    effective phantom dark energy in scalar-tensor gravity
   * MNRAS 437, 3331                    'mirage' quintessence and phantom DE from running vacuum; effective-EoS artifacts
   * Phys.Lett.B (S0370269305011573)    mimicking quintessence and phantom energy through a variable Lambda
   * arXiv:2605.27301                   2026 review: effective phantom does NOT imply a fundamental phantom field, ghosts, NEC violation, or a big rip -- and reviews the mechanisms
   * arXiv:2605.26259                   crossing the phantom divide with modified gravity
  The 2026 entry is the decisive one. arXiv:2605.27301, 'Effective Phantom Dark Energy: What
  Cosmological Reconstruction Does and Does Not Imply', states that effective phantom
  behaviour 'does not necessarily imply the existence of a fundamental phantom field,
  microscopic ghost instabilities, violation of the null energy condition by the fundamental
  stress tensor, or a catastrophic cosmic future', and explicitly notes that reconstruction
  assumes 'the FLRW framework, the standard Friedmann equation of General Relativity'.
  That IS the proposal, in general form, already published. The framework would be supplying
  one more instance of a class that is already named, reviewed, and understood.
  [OK] 6 independent prior-art entries for the mechanism class, including a 2026 review devoted to exactly this point

====================================================================================================
S2. PRIOR ART, part 2 -- 'MOND/modified-inertia in the Friedmann equation' is ALSO occupied
====================================================================================================
   * arXiv:astro-ph/0301510             dark energy AS a modification of the Friedmann equation
   * S138764730500014X                  Cardassian expansion: DE density from modified Friedmann equations
   * arXiv:0712.4232                    dark matter from Modified Friedmann Dynamics (MOFD), relativistic MOND analogy
   * arXiv:2012.03446 / Symmetry 13,174 extended Friedmann equations introducing MILGROM'S ACCELERATION CONSTANT to give accelerated expansion without dark energy
   * MNRAS 356, 475                     observational constraints on modifications of the Friedmann equation
  The fourth entry is the one that hurts most: extended Friedmann equations that introduce
  MILGROM'S ACCELERATION CONSTANT to produce accelerated expansion WITHOUT dark energy. That
  is the same move with the same constant. Different derivation (fractional derivatives vs a
  nonlocal inertia kernel), but the same claim shape and the same physical input.
  [OK] the MOND-in-Friedmann route is independently occupied, so the novelty cannot be rescued by saying 'but ours is modified INERTIA specifically'
  NOTE the recurrence: arXiv:0809.2790 -- the Gnedin paper this corpus already retired its
  CMB lane for -- surfaced AGAIN in this search. The same prior art keeps being the wall.

====================================================================================================
S3. WHAT IS *NOT* OCCUPIED, stated precisely so it is not oversold
====================================================================================================
  The searches did NOT surface: a ZERO-PARAMETER version in which the acceleration scale is
  FIXED by the dark-energy density (a0 = kappa c sqrt(G rho_Lambda), kappa = 1/2), Lambda is
  held CONSTANT, and the resulting distance tilt is PREDICTED rather than fitted.
  That specificity is the only possible novelty, and it is worth exactly as much as the
  calculation that shows it lands. Absent that calculation it is a member of a known class
  with a suggestive coefficient -- which is not publishable and would be refereed as such.
  Also unoccupied per the search: MOND/modified inertia applied to the DESI DR2 result
  specifically. That is a gap in the literature, but a narrow one, and it is narrow because
  the general answer is already known, not because nobody thought of it.
  [OK] the surviving novelty is scoped to the zero-parameter claim, contingent on an unbuilt calculation, and not inflated

====================================================================================================
S4. *** THE MUTUAL EXCLUSIVITY -- this session produced TWO CONTRADICTORY claims ***
====================================================================================================
  BRANCH A (mi_crispy_dark_matter_ledger_2026.py): Lambda EVOLVES as DESI says, therefore
    a0 evolves, therefore LCDM must absorb an A(z). The whole ledger presumes evolving rho_DE.
  BRANCH B (mi_phantom_artifact_2026.py): Lambda is CONSTANT and the apparent evolution is an
    artifact of standard-inertia fitting, therefore a0 is CONSTANT.
  These cannot both be true. They make OPPOSITE predictions for the same observable:
      z   BRANCH A a0(z)/a0(0)   BRANCH B   separation (dex)
    0.0                 1.0000     1.0000             0.0000
    0.5                 1.0303     1.0000             0.0130
    1.0                 0.9749     1.0000             0.0110
    2.0                 0.8369     1.0000             0.0773
    3.0                 0.7229     1.0000             0.1409
  So the framework's own two readings are DISCRIMINATED by a high-z RAR normalisation
  measurement -- which is a genuine silver lining: the contradiction is TESTABLE rather than
  merely embarrassing.
  [OK] the two branches separate by 0.077 dex at z=2 and 0.141 dex at z=3, so a ~0.05 dex high-z RAR measurement decides which reading the framework is allowed to hold
  CONSEQUENCE FOR PUBLICATION: neither branch can be written up while the other stands.
  A referee comparing the two documents would find the framework claiming Lambda evolves in
  one and is constant in the other, with a0 doing opposite things, and would stop reading.
  The framework must CHOOSE, in the open, before either goes anywhere.

====================================================================================================
S5. THE SYSTEMATICS-SIDE COMPETITION (already in the literature, before any new physics)
====================================================================================================
   * search-surfaced claim              the DESI hint for dynamical DE is biased by low-redshift supernovae
   * MNRAS 548, stag632                 DES SN reanalysis with updated Type Ia calibration (Dovekie): 4.2 -> ~3.2 sigma
  Combined with the amplitude computed earlier -- the whole SN-inclusive phantom signal is a
  0.025-0.069 mag distance-modulus tilt against ~0.15 mag per-SN intrinsic scatter -- the
  mundane explanations are not merely alive, they are ahead: a recalibration ALREADY moved
  DES by 0.8 sigma. Any new-physics account has to beat that, not merely match the data.
  [OK] the mundane competition is stated as ahead, not as an also-ran

====================================================================================================
VERDICT
====================================================================================================
  1. NOT NOVEL. 'Modified gravity/inertia produces effective phantom DE' has a large
     literature including a 2026 review (arXiv:2605.27301) devoted to precisely the point.
     'Milgrom's a0 in a modified Friedmann equation replacing dark energy' is separately
     occupied (arXiv:2012.03446 and the Cardassian/MOFD line). RECOMMEND AGAINST publication
     of the idea as such. Same call, same reason, as the CMB lane on 2026-07-29.
  2. The only surviving novelty is the ZERO-PARAMETER version, and it is worth nothing until
     the MI linear cosmology exists and is shown to land inside the DESI contours with
     Lambda constant. That is a real calculation, now unblocked by the 570% -> 7.9% closure
     narrowing, and it is the right next piece of work -- but as a TEST, not as a claim.
  3. THE SESSION'S TWO COSMOLOGICAL CLAIMS ARE MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE. Branch A needs Lambda to
     evolve; Branch B needs it constant. The framework has to pick one. Silver lining: a
     high-z RAR normalisation at ~0.05 dex decides it (0.077 dex separation at z=2,
     0.141 at z=3), so this is an experiment rather than a dilemma.
  4. On the amplitude, the mundane explanation is currently AHEAD, and the framework should
     not stake a claim on a signal that photometric recalibration is still moving by the
     same amount as the signal itself.

  WHAT I'D DO WITH IT: keep Branch B as the framework's PREFERRED reading, because it keeps
  a0 constant (which the low-z RAR independently prefers), it removes the ledger's
  circularity, and it stops the framework riding a feature the field calls unphysical. Then
  demote Branch A's ledger from a prediction to a CONDITIONAL: 'IF Lambda evolves, here is
  the absorption LCDM owes.' That is honest, internally consistent, and still useful.
====================================================================================================
